# The Secret of Marcus Greenleaf

Marcus Chen had learned to hide his ears very well. Every morning before school, he carefully tucked them under a knitted beanie, even in the sweltering Hong Kong summer when everyone else wore caps and complained about the heat.

"You'll get heatstroke," his friend Kelvin warned him, fanning himself with his homework folder. "Why do you always wear that thing?"

"Bad hair day," Marcus mumbled, the same excuse he'd used for three years.

The truth was far stranger. Marcus was a wood elf, the last of his kind in the city. His grandmother had brought him to Hong Kong when he was seven, fleeing from something in the ancient forests of their homeland—something he was never allowed to ask about.

School was a daily performance. In Physical Education class, Marcus had to deliberately run slower than he could. His real speed would have covered the hundred-meter dash in a time that would make Olympic athletes weep. When the volleyball flew toward his face, he had to *choose* to let it hit him sometimes, because a wood elf's reflexes would have caught it behind his back without looking.

"You're so clumsy," Kelvin laughed one afternoon as Marcus picked himself up from the ground, sand stuck to his cheek.

*If only you knew*, Marcus thought, brushing off his knees.

The hardest part was biology class. When they studied plant cells, Marcus felt like he was being asked to describe his own cousins. He could feel every tree in the school courtyard—the old banyan by the gate was grumpy and liked to drop leaves on students who carved their names into its bark. The sapling the Environmental Club had planted last year was lonely and afraid.

During lunch, while other students crowded into the tuck shop for fish balls and curry, Marcus sat under the banyan tree with his lunchbox. He would press his palm against the roots and listen.

"The new student," the banyan rumbled one Tuesday. "She carries sadness like stones in her pockets."

Marcus glanced across the courtyard. A girl named Sophie had transferred in last week. She sat alone, staring at nothing, her lunch untouched.

That afternoon, Marcus did something risky. He "accidentally" bumped into Sophie's desk, scattering her pencils. As he helped gather them, he whispered, "The banyan tree by the gate says hello. It thinks you need a friend."

Sophie stared at him. "That's... weird."

But she was at the courtyard the next day. And the day after that. Slowly, the stones in her pockets became lighter, and she started eating her lunch again.

"You're strange, Marcus Chen," she told him one Friday, smiling for the first time. "But good strange."

Marcus smiled back, careful not to smile too widely. His pointed teeth were another thing to hide.

That night, he climbed to the rooftop of his apartment building—twenty-two floors up—and let his ears free at last. The city hummed below, but above, he could hear the wind carrying messages from distant mountains. Somewhere far away, real forests still grew.

"One day," he whispered to the stars.

But for now, he had homework, a beanie to wash, and a friend who needed him. Being the last wood elf in Hong Kong was lonely—but Marcus was learning that even a hidden heart could help others bloom.

---

*The End* [end of text]


